WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:05.130
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:10.060
It's 102 degrees,
a pretty normal Dubai summer evening.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.126
My brother and I, we see the gold and
navy Chevy Tahoe parked around the corner.

00:00:14.126 --> 00:00:17.760
Even though we had just finished
soccer practice, we make a dash for

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.110
the AC we know is waiting for us.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:23.270
As we get closer,
we see our mom in the driver's seat and

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:25.650
our two cousins in the car waiting for us.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.640
We haven't seen them in a couple years,
so as soon as we hop in,

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.182
there are those enthusiastic hellos
that you know teenagers are famous for.

00:00:32.182 --> 00:00:33.334
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:33.334 --> 00:00:34.872
&gt;&gt; Hey, what's up?

00:00:34.872 --> 00:00:35.952
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:36.922
&gt;&gt; Not much.

00:00:36.922 --> 00:00:38.590
Cool.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:39.610
&gt;&gt; So our mom asks,

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.445
how was soccer practice?

00:00:41.445 --> 00:00:43.770
Good, but I'm really tired.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:47.570
Yeah, I know,
I have no idea how Cameron is so fit.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.345
I notice our cousins
giving us weird looks.

00:00:50.345 --> 00:00:53.300
Yeah, seriously he is the most
fit guy on the team, and

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.370
I'd been training all summer.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.680
Weird, right?

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:00.130
Well, my cousins are British, and
if you know anything about their lingo,

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:04.565
my brother and I had just been describing
how attractive our teammate Cameron was.

00:01:04.565 --> 00:01:06.150
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.500
&gt;&gt; The same words mean different things

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.300
to different people, and
it doesn't stop there.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:15.080
You'd hear all sorts of interesting things
at an international high school in Dubai.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.820
For example,
talks about who had the highest quality

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.590
phone would get weird looks
from our Korean classmates.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.950
To them, quality meant brand new.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.560
Or why my Japanese classmates
would start laughing.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.700
To them, quality meant zero defect.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:32.850
The same words mean different
things to different people.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:37.390
But let's be real, calling your teammate
hot by accident, or describing your

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:41.270
Nokia 3310 as a zero-defect phone,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.310
&gt;&gt; Is kind of funny.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.190
Well, sometimes the different
meanings of words aren't so funny.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:49.010
Sometimes they lead to
distance between friends,

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:53.590
distance in our communities,
distance in our society.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:56.780
And surprisingly,
one of these words is dialogue.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:02.070
So some of you are looking at me the same
way my cousins did that day, and

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:03.340
I can't blame you.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:06.260
For many in this room,
the following definition of dialogue

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:10.230
found in an essay titled the Power
of Dialogue, will resonate.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:14.870
It's defined as a discussion aimed
at fostering mutual insights and

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:16.640
common purpose.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.840
Sounds like a great definition, right?

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.984
Well, there's an increasing body of
research done by experts, like Jacob Hess,

00:02:23.984 --> 00:02:28.623
that focus on the negative connotations
conservatives hold of this word.

00:02:28.623 --> 00:02:30.455
It's actually pretty interesting,

00:02:30.455 --> 00:02:34.700
many people are concerned with the luck
of conservative representation.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:39.550
By some estimates, 90% of those involved
with the concept either through funding

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:43.630
it, developing it, or
advocating for it, or liberal.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:44.526
So why do I care?

00:02:44.526 --> 00:02:49.250
Well, these types of conversation have
been a fabric of my family growing up.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:51.160
I had four grandparents
that were all teachers,

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.080
both my parents have lived
all around the world.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.357
I have two younger brothers,
we're 15 months apart, and

00:02:56.357 --> 00:03:00.404
I really hate to admit this right not,
but not only are they taller than me,

00:03:00.404 --> 00:03:02.230
they're also smarter.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:05.203
&gt;&gt; The youngest is getting his PhD

00:03:05.203 --> 00:03:07.290
in geoscience.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.990
And the middle one made
the smartest decision of us all,

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.026
he decided not to go to grad school.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:12.026 --> 00:03:14.593
&gt;&gt; All of this is to say we don't have

00:03:14.593 --> 00:03:19.918
the stereotypical relationship
with conversations about values,

00:03:19.918 --> 00:03:22.526
or the world, or politics.

00:03:22.526 --> 00:03:26.230
For us, it's exciting and expected for
us to bring our different perspectives

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:29.740
to the dinning room table or
during the holidays.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:31.200
So with that kind of upbringing,

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.260
you can imagine how excited
I was to come to the GSB and

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.900
have these types of conversations with
some of the smartest people I've ever met.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.070
That all changed about a year
ago when President Trump

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:44.200
announced his immigration ban.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.110
I was really excited to understand what
my classmates thought of the issue, so

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.030
I decided to form some
small discussion groups.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:53.300
Well, fast forward to couple of
weeks to Wednesday, February 22,

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.370
I'm in Arbuckle Dining Hall talking with
one of my more conservative friends,

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:58.710
let's call him Jim.

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.800
We're talking about current events,
and eventually,

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:01.950
we start talking about the ban.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:07.110
Wow, Jim, I bet a lot of our classmates
would love to hear that perspective,

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.570
you should join one of the groups I'm
putting together in a couple weeks.

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:12.260
Sure, sounds good.

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:14.915
Who's going to be there,
what are the details?

00:04:14.915 --> 00:04:16.480
I list a couple of people.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:21.681
All right, so what's the format,
how is it going to be structured?

00:04:21.681 --> 00:04:25.710
It's going to be pretty casual,
during the evening, over pizza.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.350
It's just going to be
a dialogue on the issue.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.790
He grows silent, I'd said something wrong.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.850
I don't think that's for me, sorry.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.150
Really, why?

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:40.840
I don't know if I want to sit there and
be lectured on my views on the issue.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.545
No, no, no, no, this is going to
be a dialogue, where we try and

00:04:43.545 --> 00:04:44.600
understand each other.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:49.990
Yeah, I know what dialogue is,
I'll pass, thanks.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:52.100
I probably shouldn't
have been that surprised.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:56.010
Earlier that week, I'd sent an email to
another conservative friend with a title,

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.330
finding the right forum for dialogue.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.560
He responded with, really appreciate
the efforts to open up discussions, but

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.370
don't believe this is the right place for
me to help, sorry.

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:09.640
So why was my message so off?

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:14.119
Even though it's not required, many expect
dialogue to alter their views on issues.

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:19.140
If you are in a room and
you are participating, usually,

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.290
it's to mediate conflict.

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:25.670
And to do that it's usually required that
you find common ground, or you're looking

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:29.140
for solutions, or consensus, which
all require you to change your views.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:35.850
So if the overwhelming majority of
organizers of dialogue are liberals,

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.460
most of the time they're looking for
common ground, and

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:42.980
many times it's expected that participants
will soften or change their views.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:46.150
Hopefully now you start to see why
conservatives view this as a tool liberals

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:47.460
use to bring people to their side.

00:05:48.505 --> 00:05:51.075
So what did I decide to do about it?

00:05:51.075 --> 00:05:52.745
I decided to dive into the research and

00:05:52.745 --> 00:05:55.905
understand why this term was
alienating some of my friends.

00:05:55.905 --> 00:05:59.505
I had lunch with Keith Hennessey,
a former advisor to George W Bush.

00:05:59.505 --> 00:06:01.855
I took a class with James Fishkin,
the Director for

00:06:01.855 --> 00:06:04.075
the Center of Deliberative Democracy.

00:06:04.075 --> 00:06:07.815
I had multiple calls with interfaith
dialogue expert Randy Paul.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:12.520
If the same words mean different
things to different people,

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:15.900
research has shown that reframing
the concept of dialog can

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.890
reinforce the healthy norms of discourse
that are practiced on both sides.

00:06:21.028 --> 00:06:25.620
So armed with this view of the issue,
I was able to successfully host

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:30.030
multiple discussion groups last quarter
with not just those two friends, but

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.890
with several of our
conservative classmates.

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:36.990
I did so partly by highlighting the
following three benefits of dialogue, and

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:40.710
as you try to engage with others
in similar types of interactions,

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:42.410
I hope you can remember
to mention them as well.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:44.880
Expand your knowledge.

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:49.830
Even if you believe you possess the
objective truth, participating in these

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:53.840
types of conversations allows
you to test out your beliefs.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:56.410
And gain new and
interesting perspectives and

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:58.695
information you otherwise
would not have perceived.

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:02.420
Advocate effectively,

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:06.450
those that participate in dialogue
are better missionaries and advocates.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:08.830
If the world doesn't see an issue
in the way you would like, or

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:12.720
in the right way, participating in this
discussion will help you understand why.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:19.610
Own your story, dialogue is an excellent
platform for people to become their own

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:24.440
platform, and not just defined by others
or organizations, like mainstream media.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:30.830
The problem is as we shy away from
these types of conversations,

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:35.700
we have false assumptions
that certain groups speak for

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:42.030
an entire movement, or that false
assumptions continue to persist.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:45.680
So for example,
ISIS does not represent all of Islam, and

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:49.340
neither does one conservative
movement represent the entire camp.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.810
This, though, is lost when we aren't
the ones talking about our own personal

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:54.400
stories in these types of conversations.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:01.170
So different meanings of words
can lead to some funny stories.

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.097
And sometimes it can lead to distance.

00:08:03.097 --> 00:08:05.400
Or other times,
they're the foundation of a country.

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:10.429
We hold these truths to be self-evident,
that all men are created equal,

00:08:10.429 --> 00:08:14.103
that they are endowed by their creator
with certain unalienable rights.

00:08:14.103 --> 00:08:19.099
The phrase all men are created equal
means something different to us

00:08:19.099 --> 00:08:22.439
today than it meant to
our Founding Fathers.

00:08:22.439 --> 00:08:26.773
And that's okay, they had different ideas,
different assumptions,

00:08:26.773 --> 00:08:30.120
different connotations, but
they worked through it.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:35.306
They did so by defining those
differences and the benefits of them.

00:08:35.306 --> 00:08:38.840
Today, let's do the same thing.

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:42.550
Let's realize that dialogue can mean
different things to different people.

00:08:42.550 --> 00:08:46.270
But better yet, let's reframe
the concept in a more inclusive way, so

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:47.704
we all want to participate.

00:08:47.704 --> 00:08:52.617
Doing so
will literally make us a better country.

00:08:52.617 --> 00:08:58.451
And by the way, is it just me, or
is the world literally starting

00:08:58.451 --> 00:09:02.393
to mean something different these days?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:09:02.393 --> 00:09:03.819
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:09:03.819 --> 00:09:04.910
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:06.136
[MUSIC]

